ont-family: Lato, Tahoma, sans-serif;">The Em Dash Era Is Finally Over?</sp=
an></a></span></h2>
<p style=3D"font-size:16px; line-height:175%"><span style=3D"font-family: L=
ato, Tahoma, sans-serif;">Sam Altman=E2=80=99s post announcing that ChatGPT=
 will finally stop sneaking in em dashes (if you tell it to in Custom Instr=
uctions) set off a wave of memes, grammar debates, and light existential pa=
nic across Reddit and X.</span></p>
<p style=3D"font-size:16px; line-height:175%"><span style=3D"font-family: L=
ato, Tahoma, sans-serif;">Across platforms, users test the =E2=80=9Cfix=E2=
=80=9D, arguing over why LLMs latched onto the em dash in the first place. =
Top comments floated theories that it was a training artifact, an accidenta=
l =E2=80=9Cwatermark,=E2=80=9D or even a =E2=80=9Cpoison pill=E2=80=9D to s=
top AI text from blending too easily with human writing. Others joked that =
the internet just lost its favorite =E2=80=9CAI tell,=E2=80=9D and a few in=
sisted the fix still doesn=E2=80=99t work. And then there were the em-dash =
loyalists, defending the punctuation as a perfectly normal writing choice l=
ong before ChatGPT ever existed.</span></p>
<p style=3D"font-size:16px; line-height:175%"><span style=3D"font-family: L=
ato, Tahoma, sans-serif;">Altman=E2=80=99s announcement on X went viral ins=
tantly. Most replies treated it like an oddly poetic milestone, OpenAI solv=
ing the internet=E2=80=99s pettiest annoyance. Overall: half relief, half c=
omedy, and a persistent worry that AI text just got a little harder to reco=
gnize.</span></p>
